<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aras River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Araxes, Araks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aras, ancient Araxes, is a river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>, about 1,070 kilometres long, which rises in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and joins the Kura in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Its fertile valley was the heartland of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, where the capitals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a> and Armavir stood. Since the Russo-Persian treaties of 1813 and 1828 it has largely formed the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the lands to its north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aras-river.jpg" alt="Aras River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M karzarj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jolfa-Aras2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Aras is a transboundary river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. It rises in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and flows along the borders between Turkey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, between Turkey and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> exclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and both Azerbaijan and Armenia, before it flows through Azerbaijan into the Kura as a right tributary. It drains the south side of the Lesser Caucasus, while the Kura drains the north side. Its total length is 1,072 kilometres and its watershed covers 102,000 square kilometres, which makes it one of the longest rivers in the Caucasus.</p><p>Its major tributaries include the Arpa Çayı, also called the Akhuryan, which gathers the waters of the Kars River and Lake Çıldır, the Hrazdan, which empties into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>, and the Qareh Sū from the Sabalān Mountains. The lowest point in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> lies along the Aras, at 380 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of classical antiquity knew the river as the Araxes. Armenians called it Eraskh, and its modern Armenian name is Arax or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arak-iran">Araks</a>; in Old Georgian it was Rakhsi, and in Azerbaijani it is Araz. In Armenian tradition it is named for Arast, a great-grandson of the patriarch Haik. The Hellenized name Araxes was applied to the Kura–Araxes culture, a prehistoric people of the valleys of the Kura and Aras. Virgil's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> calls the river angry at the bridge, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a bridge over it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> cites a legend that in ancient times the Araxes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> had no outflow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and spread across the plains to form a lake. Robert H. Hewsen described it as the only true river of Armenia, and as Mother Araxes, a symbol of pride to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>.</p><p>In 589, in the Sasanian era, a battle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> was fought on the river. In Islamic times it was known in Arabic as al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Rass</a> and in Perso-Turkish contexts as Aras. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> the river was chosen as the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and Qajar Iran, which was forced to cede its Caucasian territories to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Because of these 19th-century changes, one scheme that is not widely accepted draws the Aras as the line between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> built the Aras Dam near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> between 1963 and 1970, creating the Aras Reservoir. Iran later built the Khoda Afarin Dam near the historic Khudafarin Bridges, and the Giz Galasi Dam 12 kilometres downstream, between 1999 and 2008. The valley in Iğdır Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, is a wetland rich in bird species, and a bird research and education centre was founded there in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aras River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Red Army suppressed the revolt of February 1921, and the anti-Soviet rebellion of Garegin Nzhdeh in mountainous Zangezur was defeated, its leaders driven across the <b>Araks</b> River into Iran on 15 July 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained part of Qajar Iran until the Russo-Persian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828, when the treaties of Gulistan and Turkmenchay ceded the khanates north of the <b>Aras River</b> to the Russian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He seized Yervandashat and in 185 BCE founded a new capital, Artaxata, near the <b>Araxes</b> where the Metsamor joined it, by the heights of Khor Virap below Mount Ararat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A second war in 1826–1828, despite early successes, ended in defeat and the Treaty of Turkmenchay, which surrendered Erivan and Nakhichevan and fixed the frontier on the <b>Aras</b> river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1604–1605 Abbas I uprooted the merchant community of Julfa on the <b>Aras</b> river, and this deportation of Armenians to New Julfa, a suburb of his new capital Isfahan, was a deliberate economic policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cimmerians lived in the Caspian and Ciscaucasian steppes, from the <b>Araxes</b>, that is the Volga, which divided them from the Scythians, west to the Bosporus, the Kerch Strait.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
